# Review: ECHO queries

The pipeline the spec describes is built. `QueryResultImpl` reads one `LocalQueryEngine`, which owns
the `WorkingSetView` per database and the `RegistryView` and reads `IndexQuerySource` as its only
`RemoteQuerySource`. `judgeRemoteEntry` in `merge-plan.ts` gives the drop, verified or trusted
verdict per entry, and `Query.characteristics` classifies each branch. On the host, `QueryExecutor`
ships versions, paths and manifests under the 16-path, 512-edge and 256 KiB bounds, and `QueryService`
carries `unchanged` and `truncated`. The acceptance scenarios map one to one onto
`sync-query.test.ts`, `local-query-engine.test.ts` and the executor-evidence suite in
`echo-host.test.ts`, as the spec says.

The gaps are mostly untested edges: the path, edge and byte bounds (F-7.3, F-7.4, F-8.4), the
min/max widening rule (F-8.3), verification at equal heads (F-4.3), `resolution.verified` and
`resolution.version` (F-5.2, F-5.3), late-opened feeds (F-10.2), and the O(n) feed re-evaluation
(F-10.4). One partial divergence: the host's union step passes through the paths a traversal branch
recorded, so a hit inside a union can ship paths. The client still trusts it, because
`pathPlanOf` reads only a linear chain (F-7.5).

Of 61 items: 49 done, 11 untested, 1 partial, 0 diverged, 0 missing.

